\section{The two sources}{

\code{squeue} reports what is queued or running now, and it is the only source of
the pending \code{reason}. It forgets a job the moment that job ends.

\code{sacct} reads the accounting database, so it reports the final state, the
exit code and the elapsed time of a job that finished. It carries no reason.

A job that is queued or running appears in both. Its \code{squeue} row is the one
that reaches the caller, because that row carries the reason.
}

\section{Why a directory needs no R session}{

The session that submitted the chain is gone by the time anybody asks. So the
\code{dir} form reads the chain off disk, and it reads two kinds of file.

Each job that started wrote \verb{<name>_<job id>.out}, because \code{\link[=slurm_write]{slurm_write()}}
sets \verb{#SBATCH --output=<dir>/<name>_\%j.out}. Those job ids identify the run
exactly, and \code{sacct} receives them. A chain submitted twice therefore reports
the run whose logs are on disk.

A job that has not started yet wrote no log. Only \code{squeue} sees such a job,
and only by job name. The \code{.sh} files that \code{\link[=slurm_write]{slurm_write()}} wrote supply those
names.

The \code{sacct} window starts at the oldest modification time of those files, the
\code{.sh} scripts and the \code{.out} logs together.
}

\section{The pending rows come from a name match}{

A job name is not unique, so \code{squeue} MAY return a job that is not this
chain's. Another user runs a job of the same name, or a later submission of
this chain sits in the queue.
}

\section{It refuses rather than reports calm}{

A scheduler that cannot answer stops the call. An absent \code{squeue} or \code{sacct},
a non-zero exit, and a line the parser cannot read are each an error that
names the command.

The \code{-S} flag is why that matters. \code{sacct} defaults to the jobs that started
today, and it reports an empty result for a window that holds nothing. A
chain started on Monday would read as "nothing is running" on Wednesday.
Every \code{sacct} call here passes \code{-S}.

A window that genuinely holds no job is a different case, and it is not an
error.
}

\section{There is no memory column}{

\code{MaxRSS} is empty for every job on Slurm 25.11.2, and a blank memory column
reads as "the job used no memory". \code{\link[=slurm_write]{slurm_write()}} writes the real peak into
each job's own \code{.out} file, from \verb{/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.peak} with a \code{VmHWM}
fallback. Read it there.
}

